-- Randomized, mostly-asymmetric follow graph among the ~200 seeded fake
-- profiles (@example.com accounts, same uuid list as seed_avatars.sql) so
-- follower/following counts don't look artificially symmetric — a real
-- social graph has one-way follows, not everyone mutually following
-- everyone back. Generated offline with a seeded PRNG (mulberry32, same
-- generator as scripts/generate-prices.mjs) for reproducibility: each
-- profile gets a Zipf-skewed popularity weight, then draws a random
-- out-degree (5-40) of weighted-sampled following targets; any profile that
-- ended up with zero followers gets 2-4 forced inbound edges, and no
-- profile's inbound count exceeds ~55% of the pool, so nothing reads as a
-- broken one-way outlier. Not a committed generator script, same one-off
-- pattern as seed_avatars.sql's photo assignment.
-- Every one of these accounts is is_private = false, so every row is
-- explicitly status = 'accepted' (matches the column's own default and what
-- enforce_follow_request_status in schema.sql would compute anyway —
-- spelled out here for clarity, since a client insert never gets to write
-- this column directly once that trigger exists).
-- Run this once in the SQL Editor, after schema.sql and seed_avatars.sql.

on conflict (follower_id, following_id) do nothing;
